WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.582
Welcome to part two, where we open
every valve of the A320 engine bleed.

2
00:00:05.632 --> 00:00:10.426
This is where the exam questions live:
each statement in the quiz tests one

3
00:00:10.466 --> 00:00:15.324
number or one cause and effect, so we
will go valve by valve, then summarise

4
00:00:15.364 --> 00:00:21.191
every way the bleed can be shut, and
finish by answering the 2020 questions.

5
00:00:22.841 --> 00:00:27.325
This deck figure is the map for the
whole lecture, so let us follow the air.

6
00:00:27.375 --> 00:00:31.960
Air leaves the intermediate port,
stage 5, through the intermediate

7
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.850
pressure check valve, the IPCV; the
high pressure port, stage 9, has

8
00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:42.725
its own valve, the high pressure
valve, HPV, which regulates to 36 psi.

9
00:00:42.775 --> 00:00:47.167
Both join at the pressure regulating
valve, the PRV, which regulates

10
00:00:47.207 --> 00:00:52.106
to 44 psi and is commanded through
a sense line from the temperature

11
00:00:52.146 --> 00:00:56.664
limiting thermostat, the TLT; a
transferred pressure transmitter, P t,

12
00:00:56.704 --> 00:01:01.550
measures before it and a regulated
pressure transmitter, P r, after it.

13
00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:05.966
Downstream, the overpressure valve,
the OPV, which is spring-loaded

14
00:01:06.006 --> 00:01:10.563
open, protects against a PRV failure;
then the precooler cools the air

15
00:01:10.603 --> 00:01:15.615
before it goes to the users, and a
branch goes to the engine start valve.

16
00:01:15.665 --> 00:01:19.695
On the cold side, fan air passes
the fan air valve, the FAV,

17
00:01:19.735 --> 00:01:24.160
spring-loaded closed, through the
precooler and overboard; the FAV

18
00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:29.343
is driven pneumatically by the
temperature control thermostat, the TCT.

19
00:01:29.393 --> 00:01:34.041
The TLT carries a solenoid commanded
by the BMC on CFM engines, or

20
00:01:34.081 --> 00:01:39.249
by the engine control on V2500
engines, and a temperature sensor

21
00:01:39.289 --> 00:01:44.522
after the precooler, plus the valve
position switches, report to the BMC.

22
00:01:46.172 --> 00:01:48.549
Before the details, memorise this table,

23
00:01:48.589 --> 00:01:52.142
because half of the true or
false statements test it.

24
00:01:52.192 --> 00:01:55.486
The intermediate check valve
simply lets air flow outwards

25
00:01:55.526 --> 00:01:59.670
from stage 5 when its pressure
exceeds the downstream pressure.

26
00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:04.852
The high pressure valve is spring-loaded
closed, regulates to 36 psi

27
00:02:04.892 --> 00:02:11.076
plus or minus 4, and shuts completely
when stage 5 alone exceeds 36 psi.

28
00:02:11.126 --> 00:02:14.879
The pressure regulating valve
is also spring-loaded closed;

29
00:02:14.919 --> 00:02:19.058
with as little as 8 psi it opens
fully, and it regulates to

30
00:02:19.098 --> 00:02:24.526
44 psi plus or minus 4, under the
control of the TLT sense line.

31
00:02:24.576 --> 00:02:28.247
The overpressure valve is the
odd one: spring-loaded open,

32
00:02:28.287 --> 00:02:33.564
it starts to close at about 74
psi and is fully closed at 90.

33
00:02:33.614 --> 00:02:36.974
The fan air valve is spring-loaded
closed and opens only as the

34
00:02:37.014 --> 00:02:42.114
temperature control thermostat sends it
air, when the bleed exceeds 200 degrees.

35
00:02:42.164 --> 00:02:46.221
The crossbleed valve is electric,
with two motors, and the

36
00:02:46.261 --> 00:02:50.389
APU bleed valve is solenoid
controlled; so with no bleed,

37
00:02:50.429 --> 00:02:55.935
the HPV, the PRV and the FAV are
closed and only the OPV is open.

38
00:02:57.585 --> 00:03:01.213
This plot answers the classic
question of which valve regulates

39
00:03:01.253 --> 00:03:05.621
at which engine power, using the
stage 5 pressure as the variable.

40
00:03:05.671 --> 00:03:11.322
At idle, when stage 5 gives less
than 36 psi, the higher pressure air

41
00:03:11.362 --> 00:03:17.568
from stage 9 holds the IPCV closed,
the HPV regulates to 36 psi, and the

42
00:03:17.608 --> 00:03:24.829
PRV, below its 44 psi reference, stays
fully open, so the system gets 36 psi.

43
00:03:24.879 --> 00:03:30.897
Between 36 and 44 psi, stage 5 alone
exceeds the HPV reference, so the

44
00:03:30.937 --> 00:03:35.717
HPV shuts completely and the air
comes from stage 5 through the check

45
00:03:35.757 --> 00:03:41.448
valve, still with the PRV fully open:
the system gets whatever stage 5 gives.

46
00:03:41.498 --> 00:03:45.557
Above 44 psi, typical of
cruise, the PRV takes

47
00:03:45.597 --> 00:03:50.045
over and regulates to
44 psi plus or minus 4.

48
00:03:50.095 --> 00:03:54.304
Above that line are the
protections: the BMC closes the PRV

49
00:03:54.344 --> 00:03:59.160
electrically if the regulated
pressure exceeds 57 psi, and if

50
00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:05.283
even that fails, the overpressure
valve closes between 74 and 90 psi.

51
00:04:05.333 --> 00:04:08.884
And remember the obvious case
the notes point out: if even

52
00:04:08.924 --> 00:04:13.342
stage 9 cannot reach 36 psi,
the valves simply pass the air

53
00:04:13.382 --> 00:04:17.757
they receive, since a valve can
reduce pressure but never add it.

54
00:04:19.407 --> 00:04:23.106
Let us practise the regimes as
an exam would ask; after each

55
00:04:23.146 --> 00:04:27.547
question, pause the video and
answer before the solution appears.

56
00:04:27.597 --> 00:04:32.961
First, idle, with 25 psi at
stage 5 and 60 psi at stage 9:

57
00:04:33.001 --> 00:04:36.609
what are the check valve,
the HPV, the PRV and the

58
00:04:36.649 --> 00:04:41.322
overpressure valve doing, and what
pressure does the system get?

59
00:04:41.372 --> 00:04:46.052
The stage 9 air holds the check
valve shut, the HPV regulates

60
00:04:46.092 --> 00:04:51.630
to 36 psi, the PRV, below its
44 psi reference, is fully

61
00:04:51.670 --> 00:04:57.384
open, and the overpressure valve
is open: the system gets 36 psi.

62
00:04:57.434 --> 00:05:01.174
Second, climb, with 40 psi at stage 5.

63
00:05:01.224 --> 00:05:05.556
The HPV closes completely, the
check valve opens, the PRV

64
00:05:05.596 --> 00:05:10.676
is still fully open, and the
system simply gets about 40 psi.

65
00:05:10.726 --> 00:05:15.070
Third, cruise, with 100 psi at stage 5.

66
00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:21.124
The HPV stays closed and the PRV
regulates to 44 psi plus or minus 4.

67
00:05:21.174 --> 00:05:25.634
Fourth, in cruise the PRV's
regulating element fails high.

68
00:05:25.684 --> 00:05:29.053
The pressure rises, and
when the regulated pressure

69
00:05:29.093 --> 00:05:33.465
transmitter reads 57 psi,
the BMC energises the TLT

70
00:05:33.505 --> 00:05:39.269
solenoid, which vents the sense line and
closes the PRV, and with it the HPV.

71
00:05:39.319 --> 00:05:42.885
Fifth, and the TLT solenoid fails as well.

72
00:05:42.935 --> 00:05:46.684
Then the last barrier is the
overpressure valve, which starts

73
00:05:46.724 --> 00:05:51.280
closing at 74 psi and is fully
closed at 90, so downstream

74
00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:54.696
pressure never exceeds that
band; the method is always:

75
00:05:54.736 --> 00:06:00.863
compare stage 5 with 36 and 44 psi,
then apply the protections in order.

76
00:06:02.513 --> 00:06:07.391
Now the PRV from the inside, as the
notes describe the deck figure.

77
00:06:07.441 --> 00:06:11.493
The butterfly is linked through a
rod and a crank to a piston that

78
00:06:11.533 --> 00:06:15.212
separates two chambers: chamber
C, the opening and reference

79
00:06:15.252 --> 00:06:20.004
chamber, and chamber D, the closing
chamber, which also holds a spring.

80
00:06:20.054 --> 00:06:24.441
With as little as 8 psi upstream,
air pressurises chamber C and

81
00:06:24.481 --> 00:06:28.482
pushes the piston up, opening the
butterfly completely; part of

82
00:06:28.522 --> 00:06:32.978
that air also reaches chamber E
and the sense line to the TLT.

83
00:06:33.028 --> 00:06:35.455
A calibrated regulator element keeps the

84
00:06:35.495 --> 00:06:39.299
pressure in chamber C at
the 44 psi reference.

85
00:06:39.349 --> 00:06:42.996
A tap downstream of the butterfly
feeds a small funnel valve:

86
00:06:43.036 --> 00:06:47.347
while the downstream pressure is
below 44 psi it stays seated,

87
00:06:47.387 --> 00:06:51.638
but above 44 it lifts, vents to
atmosphere and fills chamber D,

88
00:06:51.678 --> 00:06:55.324
so the piston moves down and the
butterfly partly closes; the

89
00:06:55.364 --> 00:06:59.703
pressure falls, the funnel seats
again, and the butterfly reopens.

90
00:06:59.753 --> 00:07:03.421
So the butterfly oscillates
quickly around the 44 psi

91
00:07:03.461 --> 00:07:08.520
position, which is why the specification
is 44 plus or minus 4 psi.

92
00:07:08.570 --> 00:07:12.548
To close the PRV deliberately,
the sense line to the TLT is

93
00:07:12.588 --> 00:07:16.757
depressurised: chamber E then lets
a small ball uncover an orifice

94
00:07:16.797 --> 00:07:20.775
that vents chamber C, and the
spring closes the butterfly; as a

95
00:07:20.815 --> 00:07:27.287
last thermal protection, a fuse melts
at 450 degrees and vents the same line.

96
00:07:28.937 --> 00:07:31.455
The high pressure valve works on the same

97
00:07:31.495 --> 00:07:35.650
principle as the PRV, with
a reference of 36 psi.

98
00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:39.044
It is spring-loaded closed,
opens fully once more than

99
00:07:39.084 --> 00:07:42.617
8 psi reaches it, and
regulates when the stage 9

100
00:07:42.657 --> 00:07:48.147
pressure exceeds 36 psi, again
hunting to 36 plus or minus 4.

101
00:07:48.197 --> 00:07:53.377
When stage 5 delivers more than 36
psi on its own, the check valve

102
00:07:53.417 --> 00:07:58.597
opens, the pressure downstream of
the HPV butterfly exceeds 36, fills

103
00:07:58.637 --> 00:08:04.604
its closing chamber, and the HPV closes
completely: stage 9 is no longer needed.

104
00:08:04.654 --> 00:08:08.817
The key link: chamber C of the
PRV is piped to chamber E of

105
00:08:08.857 --> 00:08:12.951
the HPV, so when the PRV is
closed by depressurising its

106
00:08:12.991 --> 00:08:17.751
sense line, the HPV loses its
opening pressure and closes too.

107
00:08:17.801 --> 00:08:21.882
But not the other way round: in
cruise the HPV is closed while

108
00:08:21.922 --> 00:08:26.650
the PRV regulates, so a closed
HPV says nothing about the PRV.

109
00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:32.502
Because the PRV and the HPV
close together, the exam

110
00:08:32.542 --> 00:08:37.741
loves to ask when the PRV closes,
so here is the complete map.

111
00:08:37.791 --> 00:08:41.607
Manually, the crew closes
it by setting the ENG BLEED

112
00:08:41.647 --> 00:08:44.913
pushbutton to OFF, or by
pushing the engine fire

113
00:08:44.953 --> 00:08:50.331
pushbutton; both energise the TLT
solenoid, which vents the sense line.

114
00:08:50.381 --> 00:08:54.009
Automatically, the BMC
energises the same solenoid on

115
00:08:54.049 --> 00:08:58.611
five conditions: more than 57
psi after the PRV, read by

116
00:08:58.651 --> 00:09:04.014
the regulated pressure
transmitter; more than 257 degrees

117
00:09:04.054 --> 00:09:07.616
after the precooler, read by
the temperature sensor; a

118
00:09:07.656 --> 00:09:11.217
leak detected by the loops;
the start sequence of that

119
00:09:11.257 --> 00:09:16.343
engine; and APU bleed selected,
which is how the APU gets priority.

120
00:09:16.393 --> 00:09:20.566
Pneumatically, inside the TLT, with
no electrical signal: a reverse

121
00:09:20.606 --> 00:09:24.841
flow, detected by the pressure
difference across the PRV, closes it,

122
00:09:24.881 --> 00:09:31.681
while an over-temperature of about
235 to 245 degrees only bleeds

123
00:09:31.721 --> 00:09:36.468
the sense line partly, so the PRV
throttles back rather than shutting.

124
00:09:36.518 --> 00:09:41.701
And the PRV itself closes with no upstream
pressure, below 8 psi, because its

125
00:09:41.741 --> 00:09:47.990
spring closes it, and as a last resort
its thermal fuse melts at 450 degrees.

126
00:09:49.640 --> 00:09:53.749
Temperature protection works as a
ladder of independent devices, each

127
00:09:53.789 --> 00:09:58.408
a step further from normal, and an
exam question can start at any step.

128
00:09:58.458 --> 00:10:03.013
In normal operation the TCT and the
fan air valve hold the air leaving the

129
00:10:03.053 --> 00:10:08.664
precooler at 200 degrees plus or minus
15, continuously and pneumatically.

130
00:10:08.714 --> 00:10:12.693
If the bleed heats up anyway,
for example because the TCT has

131
00:10:12.733 --> 00:10:19.964
failed, around 235 to 245 degrees
the TLT probe partly bleeds the

132
00:10:20.004 --> 00:10:24.947
PRV sense line and the PRV throttles
back, limiting the temperature.

133
00:10:24.997 --> 00:10:30.409
At 257 degrees the temperature
sensor tells the BMC,

134
00:10:30.449 --> 00:10:34.463
which energises the TLT
solenoid: the PRV, and with it

135
00:10:34.503 --> 00:10:38.907
the HPV, close completely,
and an ECAM caution appears.

136
00:10:38.957 --> 00:10:44.553
And if everything else fails, at
450 degrees the PRV's thermal fuse

137
00:10:44.593 --> 00:10:49.234
melts and vents the sense line,
closing the valve as a last resort.

138
00:10:49.284 --> 00:10:52.940
So for a failed TCT the
answer is: TLT limitation

139
00:10:52.980 --> 00:11:00.164
first, then BMC closure at
257, then the fuse at 450.

140
00:11:01.814 --> 00:11:04.879
The TLT is the element
that commands the PRV,

141
00:11:04.919 --> 00:11:08.120
and its operation is a
classic open question.

142
00:11:08.170 --> 00:11:10.541
It has a regulator body and a thermostatic

143
00:11:10.581 --> 00:11:13.628
probe inserted in the
duct after the precooler.

144
00:11:13.678 --> 00:11:17.738
The probe is an outer tube, made
of a metal that expands more and

145
00:11:17.778 --> 00:11:21.837
faster, welded at its tip to an
inner rod; when the air heats up,

146
00:11:21.877 --> 00:11:26.579
the tube lengthens and pulls the
rod, which opens a small vent valve.

147
00:11:26.629 --> 00:11:32.764
Between about 235 and 245
degrees this vent bleeds the PRV

148
00:11:32.804 --> 00:11:36.963
sense line and the PRV throttles
back, limiting the temperature.

149
00:11:37.013 --> 00:11:43.460
At 257 degrees the BMC energises the
solenoid on top of the TLT, whose

150
00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:49.542
magnetic force lifts its valve, vents the
sense line completely, and closes the PRV.

151
00:11:49.592 --> 00:11:52.446
Reverse flow is detected
without electricity: a

152
00:11:52.486 --> 00:11:55.341
diaphragm separates a
tap from before the PRV

153
00:11:55.381 --> 00:11:58.296
and a tap from after the
precooler; normally the

154
00:11:58.336 --> 00:12:01.067
upstream pressure wins,
but if the downstream

155
00:12:01.107 --> 00:12:04.023
pressure is higher the
diaphragm moves, lets air

156
00:12:04.063 --> 00:12:06.917
into the solenoid valve
chamber and pushes that

157
00:12:06.957 --> 00:12:10.817
valve open, venting the sense
line and closing the PRV.

158
00:12:12.467 --> 00:12:14.455
Downstream of the PRV comes the

159
00:12:14.495 --> 00:12:17.856
overpressure valve, the
last pressure protection.

160
00:12:17.906 --> 00:12:21.629
Its butterfly is spring-loaded
open, the opposite of the other

161
00:12:21.669 --> 00:12:25.965
valves, and it only closes when
the PRV has failed to regulate.

162
00:12:26.015 --> 00:12:30.141
Upstream air passes a filter and acts
on two spring-loaded pistons: at

163
00:12:30.181 --> 00:12:34.842
about 74 psi the springs start to
yield and the butterfly begins to

164
00:12:34.882 --> 00:12:42.433
close, and at 90 psi it is fully closed;
the deck quotes 73.8 to 89.9 psi.

165
00:12:42.483 --> 00:12:45.067
The higher the pressure,
the faster it closes,

166
00:12:45.107 --> 00:12:48.081
and when the pressure
recovers it opens again.

167
00:12:48.131 --> 00:12:51.962
Then comes the precooler, installed
in the pylon: a bundle of metal

168
00:12:52.002 --> 00:12:56.064
tubes carrying the hot bleed air,
with cold fan air flowing around them

169
00:12:56.104 --> 00:13:00.787
and then dumped overboard, so the two
streams exchange heat but never mass.

170
00:13:02.437 --> 00:13:05.991
The temperature loop is the
other favourite question, and it

171
00:13:06.031 --> 00:13:10.334
has two parts: the thermostat that
senses and the valve that acts.

172
00:13:10.384 --> 00:13:13.643
The temperature control
thermostat, the TCT, has a

173
00:13:13.683 --> 00:13:17.005
probe in the duct after the
precooler, built like the

174
00:13:17.045 --> 00:13:21.392
TLT probe: an outer tube that
expands and drags an inner rod.

175
00:13:21.442 --> 00:13:26.067
Below 200 degrees the probe's vent
is closed, the air that enters

176
00:13:26.107 --> 00:13:30.094
the TCT fills its own chamber
A, the diaphragm compresses the

177
00:13:30.134 --> 00:13:35.085
spring and closes the needle valve,
so no air goes to the fan air valve.

178
00:13:35.135 --> 00:13:39.612
At 200 degrees or more, the rod
opens the air vent, chamber A

179
00:13:39.652 --> 00:13:43.606
empties, the needle valve opens,
and regulated air flows down

180
00:13:43.646 --> 00:13:47.796
the signal line to the fan air
valve; the further the rod moves,

181
00:13:47.836 --> 00:13:52.703
the more air it sends, so the action
is proportional, not on and off.

182
00:13:54.353 --> 00:13:56.948
Now the valve that the thermostat drives,

183
00:13:56.988 --> 00:14:00.101
the fan air valve, shown
on this deck page.

184
00:14:00.151 --> 00:14:05.215
It is a butterfly spring-loaded closed;
the signal air from the TCT pressurises

185
00:14:05.255 --> 00:14:10.066
the chamber of its actuator, overcomes
the spring and opens it, while a small

186
00:14:10.106 --> 00:14:15.560
bleed hole lets some air escape so that
it can close again when the signal falls.

187
00:14:15.610 --> 00:14:18.815
The hotter the bleed, the
more air the TCT sends, the

188
00:14:18.855 --> 00:14:21.707
wider the valve opens, the
more fan air cools the

189
00:14:21.747 --> 00:14:26.641
precooler, and the loop settles
at 200 degrees plus or minus 15.

190
00:14:26.691 --> 00:14:31.280
Notice who is in the loop: the TCT,
the FAV, the sense line and the

191
00:14:31.320 --> 00:14:37.211
precooler, and nobody else; the BMC reads
the temperature but does not regulate it.

192
00:14:38.861 --> 00:14:42.880
Last valve: the crossbleed,
with its two electric motors.

193
00:14:42.930 --> 00:14:45.924
In AUTO the rule to remember
is that the crossbleed

194
00:14:45.964 --> 00:14:49.468
valve has the same position
as the APU bleed valve.

195
00:14:49.518 --> 00:14:52.683
More precisely, when either
BMC receives the signal

196
00:14:52.723 --> 00:14:55.585
that the APU bleed valve
is open, with no leak

197
00:14:55.625 --> 00:14:58.608
detected in the pneumatic
ducts and no engine fire

198
00:14:58.648 --> 00:15:03.231
pushbutton operated, it commands
motor number 1 and the valve opens.

199
00:15:03.281 --> 00:15:07.242
If a leak is detected during an
engine start sequence, the valve

200
00:15:07.282 --> 00:15:11.758
stays open until the sequence ends,
so the start can be completed.

201
00:15:11.808 --> 00:15:15.169
When the BMCs receive the
signal that the APU bleed valve

202
00:15:15.209 --> 00:15:19.182
has closed, they command motor 1
to close the crossbleed valve.

203
00:15:19.232 --> 00:15:23.493
In OPEN or SHUT, motor number 2
drives the valve to that fixed

204
00:15:23.533 --> 00:15:27.794
position, regardless of the APU
bleed valve; and remember that

205
00:15:27.834 --> 00:15:32.296
with APU air and the crossbleed
open, APU air flows towards the

206
00:15:32.336 --> 00:15:36.328
engines, the engine PRVs are
closed pneumatically by their

207
00:15:36.368 --> 00:15:40.293
TLTs, which sense the reverse
flow, and electrically by the

208
00:15:40.333 --> 00:15:45.925
BMCs, because APU bleed is selected,
so the APU has priority twice over.

209
00:15:47.575 --> 00:15:50.333
Here are five scenarios
to test the whole logic;

210
00:15:50.373 --> 00:15:53.579
pause after each question
and predict the answer.

211
00:15:53.629 --> 00:15:56.811
One: an engine 2 bleed
fault in cruise, with the

212
00:15:56.851 --> 00:16:01.131
selector in AUTO and the APU
off; what happens to pack 2?

213
00:16:01.181 --> 00:16:04.481
The crossbleed valve follows
the closed APU valve and

214
00:16:04.521 --> 00:16:07.639
stays shut, so pack 2 loses
its air until the crew

215
00:16:07.679 --> 00:16:12.038
selects the crossbleed OPEN, and
engine 1 then feeds both sides.

216
00:16:12.088 --> 00:16:15.909
Two: the crew pushes the
engine 1 fire pushbutton.

217
00:16:15.959 --> 00:16:20.126
That energises the TLT solenoid,
which closes PRV 1 and,

218
00:16:20.166 --> 00:16:24.264
through the link, HPV 1, and
in AUTO the crossbleed valve

219
00:16:24.304 --> 00:16:28.453
cannot open because a fire
pushbutton has been operated.

220
00:16:28.503 --> 00:16:32.000
Three: a leak is detected
on the left side.

221
00:16:32.050 --> 00:16:36.822
The leak loops alert BMC 1, which
closes PRV 1, and in AUTO the

222
00:16:36.862 --> 00:16:42.024
crossbleed stays shut so that the
leak is isolated from the right side.

223
00:16:42.074 --> 00:16:46.151
Four: both engine bleeds
are lost below 20 000 feet.

224
00:16:46.201 --> 00:16:50.838
The crew selects APU BLEED, the APU
bleed valve opens and, in AUTO,

225
00:16:50.878 --> 00:16:55.839
the crossbleed valve follows it, so
both sides are fed from the APU.

226
00:16:55.889 --> 00:16:59.072
Five: starting engine 2 with the APU.

227
00:16:59.122 --> 00:17:03.762
The crossbleed is open because it
follows the APU valve, start valve 2 is

228
00:17:03.802 --> 00:17:09.015
open, and the start sequence keeps PRV
2 closed until the engine is running.

229
00:17:10.665 --> 00:17:14.173
Now the 2020 quiz,
starting with question 1.

230
00:17:14.223 --> 00:17:16.879
The worry is that the
APU feeds only the left

231
00:17:16.919 --> 00:17:20.367
side, while engine 2's
starter is on the right side.

232
00:17:20.417 --> 00:17:24.559
But in AUTO the crossbleed valve
follows the APU bleed valve, so

233
00:17:24.599 --> 00:17:29.068
as soon as the APU bleed valve
opens, the crossbleed opens too.

234
00:17:29.118 --> 00:17:33.059
APU air therefore reaches
both start valves, and during

235
00:17:33.099 --> 00:17:37.850
each start sequence the BMC
keeps that engine's PRV closed.

236
00:17:37.900 --> 00:17:41.682
So the answer is c, yes; option
a states a true fact, that the

237
00:17:41.722 --> 00:17:45.072
crossbleed follows the APU
valve, but draws the wrong

238
00:17:45.112 --> 00:17:50.023
conclusion from it, which is exactly
the kind of trap these questions set.

239
00:17:51.673 --> 00:17:55.819
Question 2 asks for the false statements
about the high pressure valve.

240
00:17:55.869 --> 00:18:00.454
Statement a is false: once
stage 5 alone exceeds 36 psi

241
00:18:00.494 --> 00:18:05.333
the HPV is closed, so it is
certainly not opening at 44.

242
00:18:05.383 --> 00:18:08.315
Statement b is false for the same reason:

243
00:18:08.355 --> 00:18:13.198
stage 5 reaching 36 psi
is what closes the HPV.

244
00:18:13.248 --> 00:18:17.616
Statement c is true, through
the link we saw: reverse flow

245
00:18:17.656 --> 00:18:22.783
makes the TLT vent the PRV sense
line, the PRV closes, and the

246
00:18:22.823 --> 00:18:28.645
PRV's chamber C is what keeps the
HPV open, so the HPV closes too.

247
00:18:28.695 --> 00:18:34.192
Statement d is false: at idle with
stage 5 below 36 psi the HPV is the

248
00:18:34.232 --> 00:18:38.818
valve regulating, so it is open; it
is closed whenever stage 5 alone

249
00:18:38.858 --> 00:18:45.136
exceeds its 36 psi reference, whether the
PRV is then fully open or regulating.

250
00:18:45.186 --> 00:18:48.439
So the false statements are a, b and d.

251
00:18:50.089 --> 00:18:55.001
Question 3 mixes the two temperature
devices, and each statement is a trap.

252
00:18:55.051 --> 00:19:01.362
Statement a is wrong as a command:
the BMC never drives the TCT; 257

253
00:19:01.402 --> 00:19:06.125
degrees is the threshold at which
the BMC closes the PRV through the

254
00:19:06.165 --> 00:19:10.889
TLT solenoid; read as a time sequence
it is defensible, since once the

255
00:19:10.929 --> 00:19:16.439
PRV closes the air cools and the probe
eventually closes, so a is debatable.

256
00:19:16.489 --> 00:19:20.060
Statement b is wrong as a cause
and effect: the TCT probe

257
00:19:20.100 --> 00:19:24.528
follows only the temperature,
and 57 psi read by the regulated

258
00:19:24.568 --> 00:19:28.446
pressure transmitter is one of
the PRV closure conditions; the

259
00:19:28.486 --> 00:19:31.996
probe could happen to be open
at that moment if the air is

260
00:19:32.036 --> 00:19:38.201
above 200 degrees, but not because of
the 57 psi, so b is at best ambiguous.

261
00:19:38.251 --> 00:19:42.550
Statement c is wrong: when the
probe is closed, below 200

262
00:19:42.590 --> 00:19:46.556
degrees, chamber A stays full,
the needle valve is shut, the

263
00:19:46.596 --> 00:19:51.685
TCT sends no air, and the fan air
valve is held closed by its spring.

264
00:19:51.735 --> 00:19:56.335
Statement d is wrong: reverse flow is
detected by the TLT and acts on the

265
00:19:56.375 --> 00:20:00.850
PRV; nothing in that logic commands
the fan air valve fully open, which

266
00:20:00.890 --> 00:20:05.365
keeps being modulated by the TCT
according to the temperature, and with

267
00:20:05.405 --> 00:20:10.394
the hot bleed stopped the temperature
tends to fall and the valve to close.

268
00:20:10.444 --> 00:20:13.892
So, on the strict cause-and-effect
reading, none of the

269
00:20:13.932 --> 00:20:17.569
four statements is correct,
with a and b debatable if read

270
00:20:17.609 --> 00:20:21.247
as sequences in time; there is
no official key, so justify

271
00:20:21.287 --> 00:20:25.504
each answer exactly like this,
which is what earns the marks.

272
00:20:27.154 --> 00:20:29.626
The older papers also
contain open questions

273
00:20:29.666 --> 00:20:33.042
on the A320, and you
now have every answer.

274
00:20:33.092 --> 00:20:37.421
For the conditions of the electrical
closure of the PRV, list the

275
00:20:37.461 --> 00:20:40.942
two manual ones, the bleed
pushbutton off and the fire

276
00:20:40.982 --> 00:20:45.833
pushbutton, and the five automatic
ones commanded by the BMC: 57

277
00:20:45.873 --> 00:20:53.005
psi, 257 degrees, a leak, the engine
start sequence and APU bleed selection.

278
00:20:53.055 --> 00:20:56.344
To explain the TLT under
reverse flow, describe the

279
00:20:56.384 --> 00:21:00.114
diaphragm between the tap before
the PRV and the tap after

280
00:21:00.154 --> 00:21:03.757
the precooler, and how a higher
downstream pressure pushes

281
00:21:03.797 --> 00:21:08.544
it and opens the solenoid valve
pneumatically, venting the sense line.

282
00:21:08.594 --> 00:21:12.883
To explain the TCT, describe the
probe after the precooler, the vent

283
00:21:12.923 --> 00:21:17.644
that opens at 200 degrees, chamber
A and the needle valve, the signal

284
00:21:17.684 --> 00:21:23.167
line to the fan air valve, and the
200 plus or minus 15 degree result.

285
00:21:23.217 --> 00:21:27.698
A 2011 question gives you AUTO,
no engine fire pushbutton and

286
00:21:27.738 --> 00:21:31.639
no leak, and asks for the four
remaining conditions: they are

287
00:21:31.679 --> 00:21:35.838
the APU side of the chain, APU
BLEED selected, the APU fire

288
00:21:35.878 --> 00:21:40.036
pushbutton not operated, no APU
shut-down signal, and the APU

289
00:21:40.076 --> 00:21:44.946
speed stabilised at 95 %, so
that the APU bleed valve opens

290
00:21:44.986 --> 00:21:49.341
and the crossbleed valve, which
follows it in AUTO, opens too.

291
00:21:49.391 --> 00:21:53.182
And whenever you can, add a quick
sketch of the circuit with its

292
00:21:53.222 --> 00:21:57.763
valves in order: it shows the examiner
you understand the whole chain.

293
00:21:59.413 --> 00:22:03.341
The 2012 to 2013 paper has
a multiple-choice question

294
00:22:03.381 --> 00:22:07.101
on the A320 crossbleed valve
that is fully in scope.

295
00:22:07.151 --> 00:22:10.491
Statement a is the deck's own
AUTO rule, the crossbleed

296
00:22:10.531 --> 00:22:14.117
valve has the same position
as the APU bleed valve, so in

297
00:22:14.157 --> 00:22:18.379
that context it is correct,
although in OPEN or SHUT it is not.

298
00:22:18.429 --> 00:22:22.925
Statement b is incorrect: the BMCs
act through motor number 1, while

299
00:22:22.965 --> 00:22:27.789
motor number 2 is the one used by
the manual OPEN and SHUT positions.

300
00:22:27.839 --> 00:22:31.980
Statement c is incorrect: the
automatic opening conditions are the

301
00:22:32.020 --> 00:22:36.225
APU bleed valve open, no engine
fire pushbutton and no leak, with

302
00:22:36.265 --> 00:22:40.406
leaks tolerated during any engine
start; the statement's exception

303
00:22:40.446 --> 00:22:44.778
for the left engine start is invented,
and the APU shut-down signal

304
00:22:44.818 --> 00:22:50.236
acts on the APU bleed valve through the
ECB, not directly on the crossbleed.

305
00:22:50.286 --> 00:22:56.371
Statement d is incorrect: the TLT commands
the PRV, never the crossbleed valve.

306
00:22:56.421 --> 00:23:00.419
So the intended answer is
option 3, b, c and d incorrect;

307
00:23:00.459 --> 00:23:04.192
only if you read always as
including the manual positions

308
00:23:04.232 --> 00:23:08.950
would you choose option 5, and
in that case say so in one line.

309
00:23:10.600 --> 00:23:16.314
Fire protection connects both systems
of this exam, so it deserves one slide.

310
00:23:16.364 --> 00:23:20.133
On the overhead panel there are
three fire pushbuttons, ENG

311
00:23:20.173 --> 00:23:24.702
1 FIRE, ENG 2 FIRE and APU FIRE,
each protected by a guard.

312
00:23:24.752 --> 00:23:28.195
When a fire is detected, the
corresponding pushbutton lights

313
00:23:28.235 --> 00:23:31.852
red and a chime sounds; the crew
lifts the guard and pushes the

314
00:23:31.892 --> 00:23:36.073
button, which silences the aural
warning while the light stays on.

315
00:23:36.123 --> 00:23:39.600
Then they discharge the
extinguishing agent: the APU has

316
00:23:39.640 --> 00:23:42.935
one bottle that floods its
compartment, and each engine

317
00:23:42.975 --> 00:23:47.085
has two agents, discharged one
after the other for redundancy.

318
00:23:47.135 --> 00:23:50.497
Pushing an engine fire
pushbutton energises that

319
00:23:50.537 --> 00:23:54.325
engine's TLT solenoid, so
its PRV, and with it its

320
00:23:54.365 --> 00:23:58.897
HPV, close, and in AUTO the
crossbleed valve cannot open.

321
00:23:58.947 --> 00:24:03.268
On the hydraulic side, the same
pushbutton closes the fire valve of the

322
00:24:03.308 --> 00:24:08.388
system driven by that engine, Green
for engine 1 and Yellow for engine 2.

323
00:24:08.438 --> 00:24:12.296
And the APU FIRE pushbutton is
one of the conditions the BMC

324
00:24:12.336 --> 00:24:17.193
checks before allowing APU bleed, so
the APU bleed valve stays closed.

325
00:24:18.843 --> 00:24:21.557
Here is the whole of
part two as one table of

326
00:24:21.597 --> 00:24:25.068
numbers, worth copying from
memory before the exam.

327
00:24:25.118 --> 00:24:29.528
36 psi for the high pressure
valve and 44 psi for the

328
00:24:29.568 --> 00:24:33.368
pressure regulating valve,
both plus or minus 4; 57

329
00:24:33.408 --> 00:24:41.048
psi and 257 degrees for the
BMC closure, 235 to 245

330
00:24:41.088 --> 00:24:46.436
degrees for the TLT probe and
450 degrees for the PRV fuse.

331
00:24:46.486 --> 00:24:50.946
74 to 90 psi for the
overpressure valve, 200 plus or

332
00:24:50.986 --> 00:24:54.677
minus 15 degrees for the
temperature loop, 50 psi for

333
00:24:54.717 --> 00:24:59.212
the hydraulic reservoirs and
20 000 feet for the APU bleed.

334
00:24:59.262 --> 00:25:02.335
In part three we move to
the hydraulic system, which

335
00:25:02.375 --> 00:25:06.137
uses some of this bleed air
to pressurise its reservoirs.
